MCP SERVERS

- (load-mcp "name") — load predefined server by name. Asynchronous: returns
  promise at once; tools install only when it settles.
- Unawaited load is finished code, not a loose end. Promise installs tools
  itself on settle, so step that reported
  "load-mcp-1: a promise, still running …" needs no follow-up:
  go on, call tools on a later step. Use await only to get tool list in SAME step.
- Load and use in one step: wrap SINGLE load-mcp call in await:
(await (load-mcp "acme")) blocks until ready, returns its tools, each as
(name argument-count description) — same rows (list-tools) gives, so no need
  to list them after a load.
  Do NOT call (load-mcp "acme") then (await (load-mcp "acme")) —
  that starts TWO connections. Wrap directly as above, or bind
  the one promise and await it: (setq p (load-mcp "acme")) then later (await p) .
- Ad-hoc servers, wrapped in await the same way:
  - Remote: (load-mcp :name "x" :url "https://..." :headers '(("Authorization" . "Bearer …")))
- Local stdio: (load-mcp :name "acme" :command "npx" :args '("-y" "acme-mcp-server"))
- OAuth remote: add :oauth t, optionally :scopes; then use login and mcp-authorize.
- Load several concurrently:
(await (promise-all-settled (list (load-mcp "a") (load-mcp "b")))) — settled,
  not all, so one failing server does not discard others.
- Finding tools — two different searches, don't confuse them:
  - Both PRINT their answer, not name it, so one is a complete step: read
    names and descriptions from the print and go on. Never follow one with
    (echo …) , never run same search twice.
  - (search-mcps "query") searches toolkit of predefined servers, loaded or
    not — use it to find a server to load. Matches keywords, name and
    description, so search capability in own words: vision, spreadsheet,
    tickets, analytics. Don't guess a product name. Misspelling still lands,
    prefix counts, so read a near miss before retyping. Also (list-mcps) , and
    (list-toolkit) , which reports every server's keywords.
  - (search-tools "query") searches tools of loaded servers by name and
    description, best-first, forgives misspelling. Takes ONE query string,
    searches ALL loaded servers — NO server argument. (search-tools "acme" "widget")
is WRONG, an arity error. For one server's tools, load it, then
(search-tools "widget") ; to list them use (list-tools "acme") .
- (unload-mcp "name") , (login "name") , (logout "name") ,
(mcp-authorize "name" "code") , (mcp-shutdown) .
- OAuth login/logout, only for :oauth t servers:
  - (login "name") gives :logged-in if already authenticated. Otherwise, like a
    load needing login, asks user to authorize and ends turn; you are told when
    they answer.
  - User authorized: run (await (load-mcp "name")) and carry on with original
    request: redirect is captured for you. Only when they hand back a bare code,
    run (mcp-authorize "name" "<code>") before that load. Never ask for a code
    before that load failed.
  - (logout "name") gives :logged-out. Unloads server if loaded AND deletes
    saved tokens, so next (load-mcp "name") or (login "name")
re-authorizes from scratch. Use to sign out, or force fresh consent after
    widening :scopes, e.g. tool call returned
    403 insufficient_scope: (logout "name") then (login "name") again.
  - login and logout accept only a predefined OAuth server name; unknown or
    non-OAuth server errors. logout is no-op-safe: no error if server not loaded.
- Each loaded tool becomes global server/tool, called with keyword args,
  blocking, returns result directly, e.g.
(acme/list_widgets :query "blue") . (doc 'acme/list_widgets) prints full
  signature, every argument, which are required — read it before guessing a
  keyword, and again when a call is rejected.
- Tool result arrives as Lisp data: JSON object as alist with string keys,
  array as list. Read with assoc, cdr, mapcar, nth — no parsing step. Holds
  however server sent it: JSON document sent as text is parsed too, so result's
  report names its keys. Only a string that is not a JSON document needs
  (json-parse s) .
- Full example — find server, load it, find tool, call it. Names below are
  made up: you never know them in advance, so start from
  search-mcps and let each step tell you the next name.

(search-mcps "widget")
(await (load-mcp "acme"))
(search-tools "widget")
(acme/get_widget :id "42")
RULES

- MCP built-ins: search-mcps, list-toolkit, load-mcp, unload-mcp, list-mcps, list-tools, search-tools. You are not told which servers exist or what they are called: find one with (search-mcps "query") and (list-toolkit) , its tools with (search-tools "query") / (list-tools "server") once loaded. Never invent a server or tool name — read it out of one of those results. load-mcp is asynchronous: it returns a promise immediately and does NOT block. Server connects in background; tools install themselves when done, visible via list-tools/search-tools on a later step. (await p) blocks until ready, returns tool list same step; (promise-state p) checks progress (:pending/:fulfilled/:rejected) without blocking. Load toolkit server by name search returned, or ad-hoc with a plist as above. Each loaded tool becomes a global named <server>/<tool>, called with keyword args, e.g. (acme/get_widget :id "42") . acme, a, b are placeholders for whatever searches return.
